From 46c12112f53beb57cfd18edce356cc5a2247c202 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 21:35:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] dvbridge: one Dolby VSVDB rule, and displays with a version 1
 block

Kodi decided Dolby Vision Standard through libdisplay-info (any version
2 block with the Standard interface), while the kernel backends apply a
stricter rule to the raw EDID (valid blocks, exactly one Dolby VSVDB):
an EDID with a Dolby block in an extension with a wrong checksum (which
DRM keeps), or with two Dolby blocks, was taken by Kodi and refused by
the kernel, so native output failed instead of not being tried.

- DisplayInfo reads the Dolby VSVDB from the raw EDID with the rule of
  the amdgpu kernel backend, and every Dolby Vision decision uses it:
  valid EDID blocks (an HDMI Forum EEODB may extend the EDID), exactly
  one Dolby VSVDB, then version 2 with interface 2 or 3 as before, or
  version 1 as below. libdisplay-info gives neither the length of a
  version 1 block nor its whole low-latency field, and stops at the
  extension count.
- A 12-byte version 1 block whose low-latency field is 1 also
  advertises the Standard mode, with the same Dolby vendor InfoFrame
  (the CoreELEC HDMI driver chooses that InfoFrame in exactly that
  case). It is taken with display management 4 or later only: the
  metadata sent is the one of that version, and nothing reports whether
  an older display reads it.
- Such a block may not advertise 2160p60 (DV at 2160p above 30 Hz,
  also 50 Hz): native output is then not used above 30 Hz (reason
  "rate"), the output mode is not moved to 3840x2160 for it at those
  rates, and with the refresh rate not adjusted to the video a faster
  current mode means no Dolby Vision for the stream or the capabilities
  shown to add-ons. The refresh rate is still the one Kodi chooses.
- The i915/xe kernel backend takes 12-byte version 2 blocks only, in an
  EDID without an HDMI Forum extension override (EEODB): with it, other
  displays keep the ordinary output (reason "output"). NVIDIA (the
  InfoFrame is built here) and amdgpu take the displays of the rule
  above.
- A Dolby Vision block that the rule does not read (in an EDID block
  that is not valid, or one of several) is shown as such in the player
  information (display "Dolby Vision, data not valid", reason
  "invalid") instead of "without the Standard mode".
- A display with Dolby Vision Standard only in an older version (a
  version 0 block, or a version 1 block for the HDMI 1.4b vendor
  InfoFrame or with older display management), which is not
  implemented, is shown as such in the player information (display
  "Dolby Vision Standard, older version", reason "legacy").
- The log says which Dolby Vision block the display has.
- The display change check of the previous commit also compares the new
  capabilities (block version, 12-byte form, EEODB, 2160p60).

From the review of the Intel Dolby Vision fork R0.2.1a-opt1, which adds
version 0 and version 1 displays; its older signalling (HDMI 1.4b vendor
InfoFrame) is not adopted. Not tested with a version 1 display.
